<?jelly escape-by-default='true'?>
<j:jelly xmlns:j="jelly:core" xmlns:l="/lib/layout">
  <div class="alert alert-warning">
    <strong>Pipeline Metrics</strong> is using its embedded SQLite storage backend, but
    <code>$JENKINS_HOME/pipeline-metrics</code> appears to be on a network filesystem
    (<j:if test="${it.environmentHint() != ''}">${it.environmentHint()} </j:if>detected via the
    filesystem type). SQLite's write-ahead log depends on file-locking behavior that many network
    filesystems (NFS, EFS, CIFS, ...) do not implement reliably — this can cause write stalls or
    data corruption rather than just slower performance. It is also not safe if more than one
    Jenkins controller process ever shares this data.
    <br/>
    Consider switching to an external PostgreSQL or MySQL/MariaDB backend under
    <a href="${rootURL}/manage/configure">Manage Jenkins &#8250; System &#8250; Pipeline Metrics
    &#8250; Storage backend</a>.
  </div>
</j:jelly>
